msgstr ""

#: folio/dpi-h-ra/ch3-the-reference-architecture-for-d/p-3-5-1-022c9f.md:1
msgid "A defining feature of the core health registries is that they are described as authoritative, canonical sources, governed at national level. The guidance describes them at two levels. At the policy and governance level, a registry is the single authoritative source for its domain. At the implementation level, a country may hold it centrally, federate it across systems, or combine the two; the architecture does not prescribe a centralised implementation. The same distinction separates a shared metadata component from an operational financing system: the Benefits Package Registry, for example, is the authoritative reference publication of benefit package definitions, covered services and products, general scheme entitlement criteria, cost-sharing parameters, and machine-readable coverage rules where appropriate — a DPI-H component — whereas beneficiary enrolment and membership records, person-specific eligibility determination, provider empanelment and contracting, negotiated tariff and fee-schedule management, prior authorisation, executable claims adjudication, and payment initiation and settlement remain operational processes in functional applications outside the DPI-H (such as Contract and Benefits Management Systems and Beneficiary Registries; see Appendix C for a detailed boundary comparison)."
msgstr ""

#: folio/dpi-h-ra/ch3-the-reference-architecture-for-d/p-3-5-2-2ca2cf.md:2
msgid "a definition and description;"
msgstr ""

#: folio/dpi-h-ra/ch3-the-reference-architecture-for-d/t-3-1.md:10
msgid "A deployable, modular piece of software that provides a defined set of functionality and holds the data it manages, exposing both through services."
msgstr ""

