#: folio/dpi-h-ra/ch3-the-reference-architecture-for-d/p-3-2-00e672.md:1
msgid "A reader concerned with health-system function — for example, which capabilities a given goal depends on — will look first to the goal and capability views."
msgstr ""

#: folio/dpi-h-ra/ch3-the-reference-architecture-for-d/p-3-2-00e672.md:3
msgid "A reader concerned with motivation — who benefits, and why a goal matters — will look to the views that relate stakeholders, goals, outcomes, and value. \\cite{archimate-3-2}"
msgstr ""

#: folio/dpi-h-ra/ch3-the-reference-architecture-for-d/p-3-2-00e672.md:2
msgid "A reader concerned with realisation — how a capability is provided, by which components, and over which data — will look to the layered views that connect capabilities to services, components, and data."
msgstr ""

#: folio/dpi-h-ra/ch3-the-reference-architecture-for-d/p-3-7-5-341a32.md:1
msgid "A reference element is covered when at least one country element realises it. The country architecture inherits that element's criteria — the Requirements, Constraints, and conformance expectations attached to it — which are then tested as in Conformance at the Application Component level."
msgstr ""

#: folio/dpi-h-ra/ch3-the-reference-architecture-for-d/p-3-7-5-341a32.md:3
msgid "A reference element that no country element realises is treated as absent — a gap, surfaced exactly as under Planning testing from the architecture."
msgstr ""

#: folio/dpi-h-ra/ch3-the-reference-architecture-for-d/p-3-6-1-93fed1.md:1
msgid "A related distinction matters for clarity. Connecting two digital applications directly through an agreed message format achieves integration, but not necessarily interoperability. Interoperability, in its true sense, rests on open, publicly governed specifications that any conforming application component can implement, enabling one-to-many reuse and economies of scale rather than a proliferation of bilateral connections that accumulate technical debt and do not scale nationally. The objective of the architecture is interoperability in this stronger sense: shared specifications, openly governed, to which systems conform at their boundaries."
msgstr ""

#: folio/dpi-h-ra/ch3-the-reference-architecture-for-d/t-3-3.md:10
msgid "A required service serves the component that consumes it"
msgstr ""

#: folio/dpi-h-ra/ch3-the-reference-architecture-for-d/t-3-3.md:5
msgid "A service realises (provides) a capability"
msgstr ""

#: folio/dpi-h-ra/ch3-the-reference-architecture-for-d/t-3-2.md:7
msgid "A service serves the stakeholders and other elements that use it"
msgstr ""

#: folio/dpi-h-ra/ch3-the-reference-architecture-for-d/t-3-3.md:11
msgid "A service serves the stakeholders that use it"
msgstr ""

#: folio/dpi-h-ra/ch3-the-reference-architecture-for-d/p-3-7-2-25f659.md:1
msgid "A service the actor offers is one it realises — a service it exposes, which in turn serves its consumers. This is the responder role in an interaction."
msgstr ""

#: folio/dpi-h-ra/ch3-the-reference-architecture-for-d/p-3-7-2-25f659.md:2
msgid "A service the actor requires is one that serves the actor — a capability it depends on. This is the initiator role in an interaction."
msgstr ""

#: folio/dpi-h-ra/ch3-the-reference-architecture-for-d/p-3-6-4-a6fcda.md:1
msgid "A shared, internationally aligned patient summary specification, such as the International Patient Summary (IPS), should define the meaningful minimum exchanged for care."
msgstr ""

#: folio/dpi-h-ra/ch3-the-reference-architecture-for-d/p-3-7-5-341a32.md:2
msgid "A single country system may realise several reference components; it covers all of them and is subject to each one's criteria."
msgstr ""

#: folio/dpi-h-ra/ch3-the-reference-architecture-for-d/p-3-3-d4eb2e.md:1
msgid "A single layered view brings the whole picture together. Reading from the top, the health goals and the governance that frames them sit above the capabilities they depend on; below these are the functional applications through which care is delivered, and beneath them the digital public infrastructure for health — the shared components on which many goals draw. Cross-cutting enablers apply across the whole, and the foundational digital public infrastructure and technology layers provide what health builds upon. This comprehensive view is necessarily dense; the simplified per-goal views, and the worked example that follows, are the easier way in for most readers."
msgstr ""

#: folio/dpi-h-ra/ch3-the-reference-architecture-for-d/p-3-7-1-fd5750.md:1
msgid "A third, related question — whether the architecture is actually achieving its goals — is assessed by monitoring the outcomes those goals define, traced back through the capabilities and components meant to deliver them."
msgstr ""

#: folio/dpi-h-ra/ch3-the-reference-architecture-for-d/p-3-7-2-b7e354.md:1
msgid "A transaction — a fully specified machine-to-machine protocol exchange, with defined trigger events, message semantics, and expected actions — is one kind of Application Interaction: its precise, specifiable subset. The architecture works at the broader level of interaction, because the same service is often reached by interactions that are not machine-to-machine transactions (see Multiple access patterns to a single service) and by exchanges not yet specified to transaction level. Where an interaction is specified as a transaction, that specification may be supplied by a framework such as IHE rather than restated here (see Alignment with IHE profiles)."
msgstr ""

#: folio/dpi-h-ra/ch3-the-reference-architecture-for-d/t-3-7-1.md:5
msgid "A transaction such as Provide and Register between a Document Source and a Document Repository"
msgstr ""

#: folio/dpi-h-ra/ch3-the-reference-architecture-for-d/t-3-3.md:20
msgid "A transaction triggers the function that handles it"
msgstr ""

#: folio/dpi-h-ra/ch3-the-reference-architecture-for-d/t-3-7-1.md:8
msgid "A user's create-and-submit action triggers the transaction"
msgstr ""

#: folio/dpi-h-ra/ch3-the-reference-architecture-for-d/p-3-6-2-5eddcf.md:1
msgid "A widely used exchange standard does not by itself guarantee semantic interoperability. Standards designed primarily for data in motion are highly extensible, and uncoordinated extension produces what are sometimes described as interoperable silos: systems that each conform to the same standard yet are unable to exchange meaning because they have extended it differently. A nationally governed information model constrains and harmonises these extensions, providing the anchor against which conformance can be defined and tested. The benefit holds whichever representational paradigm a country adopts; aligning information models at the outset improves interoperability regardless of the underlying exchange or persistence technology."
msgstr ""

#: folio/dpi-h-ra/ch3-the-reference-architecture-for-d/p-3-5-1-6060c3.md:2
msgid "Ability to expose stable, standards-based interfaces that allow any conformant system to connect to it without requiring bespoke integration."
msgstr ""

#: folio/dpi-h-ra/ch3-the-reference-architecture-for-d/p-3-5-1-6060c3.md:3
msgid "Ability to support foundational health system functions rather than programme-specific logic and serve the ecosystem as a whole rather than a single programme's needs."
msgstr ""

