#: folio/dpi-h-ra/ch3-the-reference-architecture-for-d/p-3-7-1-7c62d7.md:1
msgid "What makes this possible is that the reference architecture is not merely a narrative: its elements and the relationships between them (Tables 3.1 and 3.2) are structured and machine-readable. An architecture expressed in this way can be tested, not only described — and testing applies at two complementary levels:"
msgstr ""

#: folio/dpi-h-ra/ch3-the-reference-architecture-for-d/p-3-4-5-0ea2bd.md:1
msgid "What SGI does: five capability categories"
msgstr ""

#: folio/dpi-h-ra/ch3-the-reference-architecture-for-d/p-3-7-5-d14cff.md:1
msgid "When a country expresses its architecture in the same formal language, adoption becomes measurable by mapping each country element to the reference element it corresponds to. The mapping is a realisation relationship — a country component realises a reference component (or actor) — the very relationship already used for \"a system realises an actor\", now applied between a country's architecture and the reference."
msgstr ""

#: folio/dpi-h-ra/ch3-the-reference-architecture-for-d/p-3-7-1-aef1b3.md:1
msgid "When a country or organisation defines its digital health architecture, it does so in the context of its own health priorities, existing infrastructure, and regulatory environment. The reference architecture offers a common benchmark against which such architectures — and the systems that realise them — can be assessed, whether they were developed with the reference architecture in mind or independently."
msgstr ""

#: folio/dpi-h-ra/ch3-the-reference-architecture-for-d/t-3-5.md:8
msgid "When it is usedWhether the component is consulted at design time (when building systems), at runtime (when systems are operating), or both"
msgstr ""

#: folio/dpi-h-ra/ch3-the-reference-architecture-for-d/p-3-4-6-7842fb.md:1
msgid "Where DPI-H supports multi-country or regional health coordination, governance design requires considerations beyond the national model."
msgstr ""

#: folio/dpi-h-ra/ch3-the-reference-architecture-for-d/p-3-5-1-24f507.md:1
msgid "Where established terms are in wide use, the guidance retains them and notes the alternatives, so that countries can map their existing systems. The Client Registry, for instance, is known in some settings as a master patient index; and the term registry is preferred over catalogue for the product component, because it reflects governance and lifecycle management rather than a simple list."
msgstr ""

#: folio/dpi-h-ra/ch3-the-reference-architecture-for-d/p-3-6-4-c0012b.md:1
msgid "Where the European infrastructure illustrates federated exchange of clinical content within a region, a complementary global initiative addresses the trust on which any cross-border exchange depends. WHO’s Global Digital Health Certification Network (GDHCN) is an openly governed trust network through which participating authorities verify the authenticity and provenance of verifiable health documents issued by one another — confirming, through a shared public-key infrastructure, that a document was genuinely issued by a trusted authority and has not been altered. \\cite{who-gdhcn} It establishes trust without centralising data: the personal content of a document remains with the issuing authority, and the network conveys only the means to verify it. And it applies the same trust mechanism across many document types, keeping the trust layer separate from — and reusable across — the clinical content it secures, in the same way that, within a country, trust and transport are kept separate from the data they carry."
msgstr ""

#: folio/dpi-h-ra/ch3-the-reference-architecture-for-d/p-3-4-5-6062e4.md:1
msgid "Where the three layers describe where governance sits, the next question is what it does. Within the governance layer, five categories of capability recur across the metadata registry components. They are described here at the level of capability clusters; a fuller breakdown of the individual capabilities within each is provided in Appendix F, and the detailed specification of any one of them is a matter for dedicated standards efforts and national programmes rather than for this architecture."
msgstr ""

#: folio/dpi-h-ra/ch3-the-reference-architecture-for-d/t-3-5.md:7
msgid "Which elements are mandatory or optional, their data types, cardinalities (how many times an element may or must appear), and their relationships to other elements. A logical model specifies the rules of data structure independently of what the values in those structures mean.Example: The blood pressure model states that systolic value is mandatory (cardinality 1..1), diastolic is mandatory (1..1), measurement method is optional (0..1), and only one primary blood pressure reading is permitted per encounter. These rules are entirely about structure — the LIMR does not define what \"auscultation\" means or which methods are clinically valid."
msgstr ""

